# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""English translations for stories, glossary, tools and case studies in V2.7."""

EN_STORIES = {
"1.1":{"title":"The Night Shift and the Wrongly Accused Machine","story":"At 10:10 p.m., the packing line reported that NG had tripled. Minh, a young engineer, noticed that Machine 2 had just been maintained and immediately said, 'It must be the machine.' Shift leader Lan did not allow the machine to be stopped. She asked Minh to write three columns: what was known, what was being guessed, and what still needed to be checked. The data showed the defect on both Machine 1 and Machine 2, but only on one new material lot. Twenty minutes later, the team found that the liner thickness of that lot had shifted from historical levels. If they had followed the first judgment, they would have spent another hour dismantling the machine without solving anything.","memory":"Good thinking starts by separating facts from guesses before taking action."},
"1.2":{"title":"Three Statements in the Same Meeting","story":"In the morning meeting, three people talked about the same issue. QA said, 'Yesterday's NG was 2.8%.' PE said, 'This machine is unstable.' Purchasing said, 'Maybe the new supplier caused it.' The manager wrote the three statements on the board and marked them F – O – A. The first was a Fact because there was data; the second was an Opinion because 'unstable' had not been defined; the third was an Assumption because it had not been verified. That small exercise moved the meeting from emotional debate to a list of things to verify.","memory":"When people know which statement is a Fact, Opinion, or Assumption, meetings shift from argument to verification."},
"1.3":{"title":"The Improvement Report That Looked Too Good","story":"A team reported that defect had dropped from 1.1% to 0.3% after a new action. The slides looked excellent and everyone was ready to close the project. Hung asked one question: 'Are the sample size and model mix comparable before and after?' When the raw data was opened, the team discovered that the post-action week ran only easy models, while the baseline included difficult ones. The conclusion that the problem was 'fixed' was withdrawn. After two more weeks of tracking, the team confirmed that the real gain was 0.4%.","memory":"Critical thinking does not destroy achievement; it protects the team from a false conclusion."},
"1.4":{"title":"Two People Doing a One-Person Job","story":"At an inspection station, two operators had always stood together because 'that is how the line was launched.' A new manager asked, 'If we designed this process from zero today, what truly requires two people?' After recording the motion, the team found that only 18 seconds of rotating the jig required a second person. They designed a simple holding feature, and the rest of the work could be done by one operator. Productivity increased without speeding up the operator.","memory":"First Principles helps you question what has long been treated as 'obvious'."},
"2.1":{"title":"From Low Yield to One Small Regulator","story":"Yield fell by 2%. At first, the team proposed eleven hypotheses. Engineer Trang forced the discussion into a logical chain: where is the symptom, what does the data show, which hypothesis fits, and what test can separate the possibilities? The breakdown showed the issue was concentrated on Machine 3; the pressure test showed regulator variation; after the regulator was replaced, the defect disappeared. A problem that looked complex became a reasoning chain that could be checked.","memory":"Good logic turns 'many possibilities' into a path supported by evidence."},
"2.2":{"title":"Did the Operator Fail, or Did the System Allow Failure?","story":"An operator entered 180 instead of 108. The manager planned to issue a reminder to the whole line. PE asked, 'If a different operator did the same task, would the system stop the error?' The answer was no. The HMI accepted any value and the WI showed the value only in small text. The team added input limits, color warnings, and recipe lock. The error did not recur even after new operators joined.","memory":"Do not only fix the person; fix the condition that allows the error to happen."},
"2.3":{"title":"The Pareto Chart Nobody Could Understand","story":"A defect report used categories such as Machine 1, Night shift, Scratch, Model X, and Others. The manager looked at it for 30 seconds and asked, 'Are these categories based on the same rule?' The room went quiet. The team then separated the data into three breakdowns: by machine, by shift, and by defect type. Only then did they see that Scratch accounted for 54% and occurred mainly on Machine 1 during the night shift.","memory":"MECE is not a fancy term; it makes data usable for reasoning."},
"2.4":{"title":"One Defect, Three Causes at the Same Time","story":"Scrap increased, but no single root cause could explain it. A cause map showed that material thickness had moved slightly upward, machine pressure was near the lower edge of the window, and camera detection was weaker on dark-colored models. Each factor individually remained within limits, but when all three appeared together, the defect surged. The team adjusted the material window, machine setting, and detection rule together.","memory":"An outcome can be produced by interacting factors rather than one isolated root cause."},
"3.1":{"title":"The Fifth Why Finally Reached the System","story":"The machine stopped because a sensor had shifted. Why 1: the sensor shifted. Why 2: the bracket was loose. Why 3: the screw moved after maintenance. Why 4: torque was not controlled. Why 5: the maintenance WI did not specify torque or require a post-assembly check. If the team had stopped at 'loose bracket,' they would only have tightened the screw. Going deeper led them to change the maintenance standard so the problem would not return.","memory":"A good 5 Why does not search for someone to blame; it searches for a mechanism that can be prevented."},
"3.2":{"title":"A Fishbone That Saved a Debate","story":"White-edge defects appeared after die-cut. Production blamed material, the supplier blamed the machine, and QA blamed handling. Instead of arguing, PE drew a Fishbone and asked each group to place hypotheses under Man, Machine, Material, Method, Measurement, and Environment. Every branch then needed a test. Three days later, the team had eliminated 80% of the hypotheses and confirmed that cutting-blade condition and tension were the two main drivers.","memory":"Fishbone expands the field of possibilities; evidence decides which branch is true."},
"3.3":{"title":"One Hundred Defects, but Only Two Deserved the First Focus","story":"In one week, there were 100 defects across 17 types. The team split people across every category and none improved much. A Pareto chart showed that the top two defects represented 71% of total NG. The team concentrated resources on those two and cut total NG by nearly half in one week. The other defects still existed, but now the team had a rational order for working on them.","memory":"Prioritization does not mean ignoring; it means choosing the place that creates the largest impact first."},
"3.4":{"title":"A Good Action Is Not Yet an Improvement","story":"PE changed a parameter and defect dropped sharply for two days. Everyone celebrated and moved to another project. One week later, the night shift used the old recipe and the problem returned. The team realized they had completed Plan–Do–Check but not Act. They then locked the recipe, updated the WI, and trained operators. Only after that did the result become sustainable.","memory":"A gain that is not standardized is still only a successful trial."},
"4.1":{"title":"A Supplier Decision Cannot Be Based on Price Alone","story":"Two suppliers both quoted 1.00 USD. Purchasing preferred the one with faster delivery. QA brought variation data, PMC brought delivery history, and PE brought trial results. Once all the evidence was on the table, the faster supplier turned out to carry a higher line-stop risk. The team accepted one extra day of lead time in exchange for better stability.","memory":"The right data reveals costs and risks that unit price alone cannot show."},
"4.2":{"title":"A 50% Reduction Without Knowing the Starting Point","story":"A project stated a goal of 'reduce defect by 50%.' During review, the manager asked which week was being used as the baseline. Nobody agreed: one person used one week, another used one month, and the model mix differed. The team reset the project with a four-week baseline under comparable product mix and a specific target. Only then could everyone use the same language when discussing gain.","memory":"Without a credible baseline, an improvement percentage is only an attractive number."},
"4.3":{"title":"The Argument Between Price and Quality","story":"Purchasing chose A because it was 3% cheaper; QA chose B because Cpk was better; PMC preferred C because delivery was faster. The meeting went on without agreement. The manager required a Decision Matrix using five criteria and weights agreed before scoring. The result did not make everyone prefer the same option, but it made the reason for the decision transparent.","memory":"A Decision Matrix does not decide for people; it makes the trade-offs visible."},
"4.4":{"title":"A Small Gain and a Very Large Risk","story":"A setting change could improve yield by 0.2%, but it reduced the detection margin for a customer-critical defect. Looking only at gain made the action attractive. Once probability and impact were assessed, the customer risk exceeded the acceptable threshold. The team selected an alternative with slightly lower gain but much better protection.","memory":"A small benefit should not hide a severe consequence."},
"5.1":{"title":"Busy Every Day, Yet the Important Work Was Still Late","story":"Mai spent her days answering email, joining urgent meetings, and handling small requests. At the end of the week, the important improvement project had not started. When she placed tasks into the Eisenhower Matrix, she realized most of her time went to urgent but low-importance work. The next week, she blocked two mornings for the important project before opening email.","memory":"Important work rarely shouts. If time is not protected, urgent work will always push it aside."},
"5.2":{"title":"Ninety Minutes Without Notifications","story":"Nam needed to analyze a DOE, but every five minutes he replied to chat. After three hours he still had not finished. The next day, he blocked 9:00–10:30, turned off notifications, and told the team to call only for a line stop. Those ninety minutes produced more progress than the previous half-day.","memory":"Time Blocking does not create more hours; it protects the quality of the hours you already have."},
"5.3":{"title":"Fourteen Tasks All Stuck at 90%","story":"An engineer had fourteen actions that were 'almost done.' Every day he switched among tasks and deadlines kept slipping. The manager set a WIP limit of three. New work had to wait in the backlog unless capacity was freed. Within two weeks, the number of completed tasks increased even though the number of tasks 'in progress' dropped sharply.","memory":"Starting less work can help you finish more work."},
"5.4":{"title":"Thirty Minutes on Friday Saved the Next Week","story":"The team kept discovering late deadlines too late. The leader introduced a thirty-minute Weekly Review: calendar, waiting list, projects, and Top 3 for next week. In the first review, they discovered that a sample had to be shipped by Tuesday but nobody had arranged logistics. A small issue was solved before becoming a crisis.","memory":"Regular review turns surprises into things that can be prepared for."},
"6.1":{"title":"One-Sentence Delegation and Three Days of Rework","story":"A manager told a new engineer, 'Please lead this trial for me.' Three days later, the engineer returned with results but no agreed acceptance criteria, sample size, or report format. The manager was disappointed, and so was the engineer. The next time, they agreed on Outcome, Authority, Deadline, and Checkpoint at the start. The second trial ran smoothly and the engineer became much more confident.","memory":"Good delegation does not only transfer work; it transfers clarity."},
"6.2":{"title":"The Manager Stopped Answering for Ten Minutes","story":"Whenever engineers asked questions, the team leader usually gave the answer immediately. One day he tried asking only: 'What is the goal? What does the data tell you? What three options do you see?' At first the conversation felt slower, but within weeks engineers began bringing possible solutions instead of only bringing questions.","memory":"Coaching sacrifices a little speed today to build more capability tomorrow."},
"6.3":{"title":"'You Are Careless' and a Better Way to Say It","story":"A manager was about to tell an employee, 'You are too careless.' HR suggested using SBI instead: 'In this morning's report, the baseline was missing, so the team could not make a decision. Next time, please run through the checklist before sending it.' The issue was the same, but the employee did not feel personally labeled and knew exactly what to change.","memory":"Good feedback describes behavior and impact instead of judging the person."},
"6.4":{"title":"The Day the Senior Engineer Took Leave","story":"Only Tuan knew how to set up a critical process. When he took leave, the line lost four hours waiting for remote support. After the incident, the team built a Skill Matrix and discovered three other skills that also depended on a single expert. They created a three-month backup plan. The next time the senior engineer was away, the line continued normally.","memory":"A Skill Matrix turns a known dependency into a visible gap that can be acted on."},
"7.1":{"title":"'Improve the Defect' Is Not a Goal","story":"A quarterly KPI said 'Improve defect A.' At the end of the quarter, the team argued about whether the goal had been achieved because the defect had improved but nobody knew what 'enough' meant. The next quarter they wrote: reduce from 1.2% to ≤0.6% by 30 Nov on Line 2. From then on, every action pointed toward the same finish line.","memory":"A clear goal ends arguments about whether the work is actually Done."},
"7.2":{"title":"Twenty Meetings, but the KPI Was Still Bad","story":"A team was proud that it had conducted twenty review meetings. The manager asked, 'Which KPI improved because of those meetings?' Nobody could answer. They separated Activity from Outcome: meetings were activities; OEE, defect, and lead time were outcomes. Some meetings disappeared, but the quality of action improved.","memory":"Being busy is not the same as creating results."},
"7.3":{"title":"Waiting for a Customer Claim Before Knowing There Was a Problem","story":"The team monitored only customer claims. When the indicator became bad, the product had already left the factory days earlier. They added leading indicators: Cpk of critical dimensions, audit compliance, and alarm rate. One month later, Cpk began to fall even though no claim existed yet; the team intervened early and prevented a large defect lot.","memory":"Leading indicators create a chance to act before the lagging result appears."},
"7.4":{"title":"Daily Review for Everything","story":"One department reviewed twenty-five KPIs every morning. The meeting lasted ninety minutes and most indicators barely changed day to day. They reclassified them: operational KPIs daily, actions weekly, cost savings monthly. The morning meeting fell to twenty-five minutes and discussion became much deeper on the indicators that truly needed fast response.","memory":"The right cadence matches the speed at which the problem changes."},
"8.1":{"title":"The Conclusion Was on Slide 15","story":"An engineer presented fourteen slides of background before reaching slide 15: 'Recommend keeping Supplier A.' The manager said, 'Next time, start with that sentence.' The next report opened with the conclusion, followed by three reasons and the evidence under each. The meeting dropped from forty minutes to fifteen.","memory":"The Pyramid Principle respects the listener's time by putting the conclusion first."},
"8.2":{"title":"Twenty Slides Compressed into One Page","story":"The team had a twenty-slide FACA, but the customer still asked, 'So what is the root cause and what happens next?' PE forced everything into one page: Background, Current, Analysis, Action, Result, Next Step. Because space was limited, they removed information that did not support a decision. The final A3 was clearer than the old deck.","memory":"A one-page constraint forces thinking to become clear."},
"8.3":{"title":"The Charts Were Correct, but Nobody Understood the Point","story":"One slide contained six charts. Every chart was correct, but the audience did not know what mattered. The engineer changed the headline from 'Defect trend' to 'Two actions reduced defect by 0.7% and held the new level for ten shifts.' He kept only one chart that supported that message. The same data suddenly became a clear story.","memory":"Data Storytelling answers the question: 'So what?'"},
"8.4":{"title":"Ten People, Ninety Minutes, No Decision","story":"A customer-claim meeting lasted ninety minutes. One person retold history, another opened a new file, and the meeting ended without clear ownership. The next week, the leader sent pre-read material, wrote the Decision Needed at the top, and timeboxed the meeting to thirty minutes. It ended with four actions, each with an owner and deadline. Nobody missed the other sixty minutes.","memory":"A good meeting is designed around an output, not a duration."},
"9.1":{"title":"The Best Engineer Who Could Not Lead a Project","story":"Dung had deep expertise in one process, but cross-functional projects were difficult because he understood little about Quality, Finance, or Project Management. He drew a T for his capability: the vertical line was process expertise and the horizontal line was collaborative skill. After a year of improving data, presentation, and project skills, his organizational influence grew noticeably.","memory":"Depth creates expertise; breadth helps expertise create influence."},
"9.2":{"title":"Ten Years of Experience or One Year Repeated Ten Times?","story":"Two engineers both had ten years of reporting experience. One kept making slides the same way; the other chose one micro-skill each week, such as headlines, charts, or logic, and asked for feedback. After a year, the quality gap was large. Their years of experience were equal, but their practice was not.","memory":"Experience becomes capability only when practice is intentional and feedback is used."},
"9.3":{"title":"The Old Problem Returned with a New Employee","story":"A claim had been solved the previous year, but the knowledge existed only in the old engineer's email. After that engineer moved departments, a similar problem returned and the new team had to investigate from the beginning. This time they updated FMEA, Control Plan, and training material. The incident became system knowledge rather than personal memory.","memory":"A Lesson Learned has value only when it enters a reusable standard."},
"9.4":{"title":"Trying to Remember Everything at Performance Review Time","story":"At year-end, Linh had to write her self-review but could remember only recent projects. The next quarter she started a Career Portfolio: for each project she recorded Problem, Role, Action, Result, and Learning. At the next review, she did not have to 'tell stories about being busy'; she had evidence of impact and a visible development path.","memory":"A portfolio turns a career from vague memory into a chain of capability evidence."},
"10.1":{"title":"Three People Each Thought Someone Else Was Responsible","story":"A trial was late because PE thought PMC would order material, PMC thought Purchasing would do it, and Purchasing was waiting for PE confirmation. Nobody had acted wrongly, but no deliverable had a clear owner. The team created a RACI: PMC Responsible, PE Manager Accountable, Purchasing Consulted. The next trial had no ownership gap.","memory":"RACI closes the gap between 'being involved' and 'owning the result'."},
"10.2":{"title":"The Same Risk Score, Very Different Consequences","story":"Two risks both received a score of 12. One caused internal rework; the other could escape to the customer. The team realized that the score was a tool, not truth. They added severity rules and residual-risk review, and required separate escalation for customer-critical risks.","memory":"A Risk Matrix helps prioritize, but judgment still requires understanding the real consequence."},
"10.3":{"title":"One A3 Replaced an Entire Email Chain","story":"A project had forty-seven emails, eight Excel files, and twelve slides, yet the manager still asked, 'What is the problem?' The engineer condensed the work into one A3: Current, Target, Root Cause, Countermeasure, and Follow-up. For the first time, everyone could see the full logic on one page and a decision was made in ten minutes.","memory":"A3 is a discipline for condensing thinking, not merely a form."},
"10.4":{"title":"A Trusted External Memory","story":"Phuc managed tasks through email, sticky notes, chat, and memory. He always felt busy because he was afraid of forgetting something. He reduced the system to four places: Goals, Projects/Actions, Calendar, and Weekly Review. Fewer tools created more trust, and he no longer checked chat constantly just to remember what he still owed.","memory":"A good personal system frees the brain from remembering commitments so it can think."},
}

EN_GLOSSARY = {
"A3":"A one-page problem-solving approach that links current state, target, root-cause analysis, countermeasures, and follow-up.",
"Accountable":"The person ultimately answerable for a deliverable in RACI. Normally each deliverable should have one Accountable owner.",
"Action":"A specific activity with an owner, deadline, and expected result; different from a general idea or suggestion.",
"Assumption":"Something temporarily treated as true before enough evidence exists.",
"Baseline":"The current-performance reference point used for before-and-after comparison or improvement evaluation.",
"Bias":"A systematic tendency that can distort how data is collected, interpreted, or selected.",
"Bottleneck":"The capacity-limiting point of a system. Improving other areas may not increase total output if the bottleneck remains unchanged.",
"CAPA":"Corrective and Preventive Action: actions that correct causes of existing problems and prevent recurrence or similar future problems.",
"Cause":"A factor that creates or contributes to an outcome. Cause should be distinguished from symptom and correlation.",
"Cpk":"A process-capability index showing how well a process fits within specification limits while accounting for centering.",
"Critical Thinking":"The discipline of checking the quality of information and reasoning before accepting a conclusion.",
"Decision Matrix":"A matrix for comparing alternatives using agreed criteria and weights.",
"Delegation":"Assigning execution responsibility together with appropriate authority, standards, deadlines, checkpoints, and escalation rules.",
"DMAIC":"Define – Measure – Analyze – Improve – Control, a structured improvement cycle commonly used in Six Sigma.",
"Evidence":"Verifiable information used to support or reject a hypothesis or conclusion.",
"Fact":"Information that can be verified through data, records, observation, or a reliable source.",
"Fishbone":"The Ishikawa diagram used to broaden and group possible causes of a problem.",
"FMEA":"Failure Mode and Effects Analysis, a structured method for analyzing failure modes, effects, causes, and risk controls.",
"Hypothesis":"A testable explanation that has not yet become a confirmed conclusion.",
"KPI":"Key Performance Indicator, an important measure showing how well a goal or outcome is being achieved.",
"Lagging Indicator":"A measure that shows a result after it has already occurred, such as customer claims or scrap.",
"Leading Indicator":"An earlier signal that can predict or influence an outcome before the final result occurs.",
"MECE":"Mutually Exclusive, Collectively Exhaustive: categories do not overlap and together cover the full scope.",
"Micromanagement":"Managing at an unnecessarily detailed level that reduces autonomy and the development of the person doing the work.",
"OEE":"Overall Equipment Effectiveness, calculated from Availability × Performance × Quality.",
"Opinion":"A personal interpretation or judgment; it may be useful but should not be presented as a verified fact.",
"Outcome":"The result that needs to be achieved, distinct from the activity used to influence that result.",
"Pareto":"A method for ranking issue categories by frequency or impact to identify priorities.",
"PDCA":"Plan – Do – Check – Act, a cycle for testing, verifying, and standardizing improvement.",
"Problem Statement":"A clear description of What, Where, When, Magnitude, and Scope without embedding an unverified cause.",
"RACI":"Responsible – Accountable – Consulted – Informed, a role-clarification matrix for cross-functional deliverables.",
"Residual Risk":"The risk that remains after controls have been applied.",
"Root Cause":"A verified underlying cause that explains the mechanism of the problem and can be controlled through appropriate action.",
"SMART":"Specific – Measurable – Achievable – Relevant – Time-bound, a framework for writing clear goals.",
"SPC":"Statistical Process Control, the use of statistical data to distinguish normal process variation from special causes.",
"System Thinking":"Seeing outcomes as products of structures, interactions, and feedback across an entire system.",
"Target":"The required future performance level, with a clear metric and timing.",
"Time Blocking":"Reserving specific blocks of time for categories of work in order to protect focus.",
"WIP":"Work In Progress, the amount of work active at the same time. High WIP often increases context switching and lead time.",
"5 Why":"A method of repeatedly asking 'Why?' to move from a symptom toward deeper causes, with evidence required at every step.",
}

EN_TOOLS = {
"5why":{"category":"Problem Solving","when":"Use when the problem has a relatively linear cause chain and you need to move from symptom to a controllable cause.","steps":["Describe the symptom with data","Ask Why for the direct mechanism","Require evidence at every step","Stop at a cause that can be acted on","Confirm the action removes the failure mechanism"],"output":"An evidence-based cause chain, root cause, and directly linked action.","mistakes":["Forcing exactly five Whys","Stopping at operator mistake","Using guesses as answers"]},
"fishbone":{"category":"Problem Solving","when":"Use when the problem has many possible cause groups or when different functions see the issue differently.","steps":["Place the effect at the head","Brainstorm by 5M1E","Write specific causes","Prioritize two or three branches","Create a verification plan"],"output":"A structured list of causes and a verification plan.","mistakes":["Treating brainstormed ideas as root causes","Writing causes too generally","Failing to move from brainstorming to testing"]},
"pareto":{"category":"Data","when":"Use to select the groups creating most of the frequency, cost, or impact.","steps":["Standardize categories","Choose the metric","Sort descending","Calculate cumulative percentage","Select priorities and review severity"],"output":"A data-based priority list.","mistakes":["Using inconsistent categories","Looking only at count","Treating 80/20 as a rigid law"]},
"pdca":{"category":"Improvement","when":"Use for controlled experiments and improvements that require a learning loop.","steps":["Plan: hypothesis and target","Do: controlled trial","Check: compare with baseline","Act: standardize or return to Plan"],"output":"An improvement that has been verified and standardized.","mistakes":["Doing before Plan is clear","Checking by feeling","Failing to Act after getting gain"]},
"a3":{"category":"Problem Solving","when":"Use when a problem-solving story must be communicated concisely to managers or a cross-functional team.","steps":["Background","Current condition","Target","Root-cause analysis","Countermeasure","Follow-up"],"output":"A one-page problem-solving story with end-to-end logic.","mistakes":["Filling the form before understanding the problem","Using too much text","Countermeasures not linked to root cause"]},
"decision-matrix":{"category":"Decision","when":"Use when several alternatives must be compared across multiple criteria with trade-offs.","steps":["Confirm alternatives","Confirm criteria","Define weights","Define scoring scale","Score and run sensitivity analysis"],"output":"A transparent comparison among alternatives.","mistakes":["Setting weights after seeing the result","Using overlapping criteria","Skipping sensitivity analysis"]},
"risk-matrix":{"category":"Risk","when":"Use to prioritize risk by probability and impact before and after controls.","steps":["Define the scale","Rate initial risk","Select controls","Rate residual risk","Identify who accepts the remaining risk"],"output":"A prioritized risk list with controls.","mistakes":["Using vague scales","Ignoring residual risk","Treating the score as absolute truth"]},
"raci":{"category":"Management","when":"Use in cross-functional projects or deliverables to prevent role ambiguity.","steps":["List deliverables","Assign Responsible","Assign one Accountable","Identify Consulted","Identify Informed"],"output":"Clear roles for every deliverable.","mistakes":["Multiple Accountables","Responsible person lacks authority","Too many Consulted roles"]},
"eisenhower":{"category":"Productivity","when":"Use when tasks are too numerous and you need to distinguish importance from urgency.","steps":["List tasks","Assess importance","Assess urgency","Do/Schedule/Delegate/Delete","Review weekly"],"output":"A more realistic priority list.","mistakes":["Calling everything urgent","Failing to schedule important work","Keeping low-value work"]},
"smart":{"category":"KPI","when":"Use when a goal is vague or when the Done condition is unclear.","steps":["Specific","Measurable","Achievable","Relevant","Time-bound"],"output":"A goal with baseline, target, scope, and deadline.","mistakes":["No baseline","Measuring activity instead of outcome","No deadline"]},
"pyramid":{"category":"Communication","when":"Use for emails, slides, and management reports where the conclusion must be understood quickly.","steps":["State the conclusion first","Group two to four supporting reasons","Put evidence under each reason","Remove detail that does not support the message"],"output":"A clear, logical, easy-to-scan message.","mistakes":["Telling history before the conclusion","Overlapping reasons","Evidence not linked to the argument"]},
"skill-matrix":{"category":"People","when":"Use to identify capability gaps, backup needs, and team development priorities.","steps":["Choose critical skills","Define levels using behaviors","Assess current level","Identify gaps","Build training and backup plans"],"output":"A capability map and development priorities.","mistakes":["Defining levels by seniority","Having no observable criteria","Failing to convert gaps into actions"]},
"one-page":{"category":"Communication","when":"Use when a problem or project must be summarized on one page to support a decision.","steps":["Background","Current status","Analysis","Action","Result","Next step / decision needed"],"output":"A one-page report with a clear message and logic.","mistakes":["Packing in too much text","No headline conclusion","Decorative visuals with no purpose"]},
"weekly-review":{"category":"Productivity","when":"Use to keep a task system trustworthy and adjust priorities regularly.","steps":["Clear inbox","Review projects","Review calendar","Review waiting items","Choose next week's Top 3"],"output":"An updated commitment list and clear priorities for the next week.","mistakes":["Reviewing only late tasks","Keeping work that no longer matters","Failing to choose top priorities"]},
"sbi":{"category":"People","when":"Use when feedback must focus on a specific behavior while reducing personal judgment.","steps":["Situation","Behavior","Impact","Next step / request"],"output":"Clear feedback with lower defensiveness and a specific next step.","mistakes":["Using personality labels","Giving feedback too late","Ending without a next step"]},
}

EN_CASES = {
"case01":{"title":"Yield Drops Suddenly","category":"Problem Solving","situation":"Line 2 yield drops from 99.2% to 96.8% for two consecutive shifts. The team suspects the new material, but there is no breakdown by machine, model, or shift yet.","question":"What is the best first step?","options":["Replace the material lot immediately","Retrain the operator","Break down the data to identify the pattern","Adjust parameters across the whole line"],"answer":2,"explain":"Before taking action, locate the problem. Breakdown by machine/model/shift/lot helps separate Fact from Assumption and reduces the risk of fixing the wrong cause."},
"case02":{"title":"Delegating a Trial to a New Engineer","category":"People","situation":"A manager assigns an important trial to a new engineer and says, 'Please handle this for me.' Three days later the output does not match the expected format and the engineer must redo the work.","question":"Which important delegation element was missing?","options":["More meetings","Clear outcome, standard, and checkpoint","The manager doing the difficult part personally","Adding more people to CC"],"answer":1,"explain":"Delegation needs Outcome – Standard – Deadline – Authority – Checkpoint – Escalation. Without a clear output standard, both sides may define Done differently."},
"case03":{"title":"Many KPIs but No Improvement","category":"KPI","situation":"The department has 28 KPIs and reviews them every week, but meetings mainly read numbers. Old actions repeat and many KPIs have no clear owner.","question":"What should be prioritized?","options":["Add more KPIs","Make the meeting longer","Reduce to key KPIs and link them to owners, actions, and cadence","Review everything daily"],"answer":2,"explain":"KPIs should be few, outcome-linked, and reviewed at the right cadence. Review should create decisions and actions, not only read numbers."},
"case04":{"title":"Two Suppliers at the Same Price","category":"Decision","situation":"Supplier A and Supplier B have the same price. A has better quality history; B has faster delivery but higher variation. Functions argue from different priorities.","question":"Which tool best makes the trade-off transparent?","options":["5 Why","Decision Matrix","Time Blocking","Fishbone"],"answer":1,"explain":"A Decision Matrix makes criteria, weights, and sensitivity explicit instead of relying on personal preference."},
"case05":{"title":"Ninety-Minute Meeting with No Decision","category":"Communication","situation":"Ten people spend ninety minutes discussing a claim. People retell history and show large amounts of data, but there is no clear decision needed and no action owner afterward.","question":"How should the meeting be redesigned?","options":["Add more slides","Purpose + Decision Needed + Pre-read + Timebox + Action owner","Invite more people","Remove data from the meeting"],"answer":1,"explain":"Meeting Discipline starts with the required output. Pre-read reduces history-telling, and timebox plus action ownership closes the execution loop."},
"case06":{"title":"Too Much Work in Progress","category":"Productivity","situation":"One engineer has fourteen active actions. Most are 'almost done,' deadlines slip, and the engineer changes tasks throughout the day.","question":"Which measure best addresses the underlying mechanism?","options":["Work overtime","WIP Limit and Definition of Done","Add more reminders","Open even more tasks to use spare time"],"answer":1,"explain":"WIP Limit reduces context switching and forces completion before new work is started. Definition of Done prevents tasks from staying '90% complete' for too long."},
"case07":{"title":"Only One Person Knows a Critical Process","category":"People","situation":"A critical process has only one senior engineer who can work independently. When that person takes leave, the line must wait for remote support.","question":"What is the best management action?","options":["Ask the senior engineer not to take leave","Build a Skill Matrix and backup plan","Increase meeting frequency","Create more KPIs"],"answer":1,"explain":"A Skill Matrix makes single-person dependency visible and supports a backup-development plan by capability level."},
"case08":{"title":"The Action Had Gain, but the Defect Returned","category":"Improvement","situation":"The team changes a process parameter and defect drops for two days. One week later the problem returns on another shift because the standard was not updated and operators used the old setting.","question":"Which PDCA step was missing?","options":["Plan","Do","Check","Act"],"answer":3,"explain":"Act standardizes the new condition in WI, parameter control, training, and sustainment. A gain is not complete improvement until it is standardized."},
}
